  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Finding Step Response from a Transfer Function",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute an LTI system's unit-step response by using $X(s)=1/s$, forming $Y(s)=H(s)/s$, and applying inverse Laplace methods to recover the time-domain output.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Step Response from a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Finding Step Response from a Transfer Function",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The first-pass procedure for this section is the step-response move in Laplace form: start from the system rule $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$, then convert it into the unit-step workflow by substituting $X(s)=1/s$. This means every worked step keeps the same notation and the same order of operations: form the product, simplify, then invert.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the unit-step transform rule directly. For a unit step input, $\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t)\\}=1/s$, so the direct substitution is $X(s)=1/s$ and the step-response transform is formed as a single division by $s$: $Y(s)=H(s)/s$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)=\\frac{1}{s}H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this base case to anchor the section: if $H(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2}$, then $Y(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s+2)}$. We now have a target Laplace form to invert, but we still hold off on technique details until later steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{Y(s)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Step Response from a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Forming the Step-Response Transform $Y(s)=H(s)/s$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $Y(s)$ for a unit-step input when $H(s)=\\frac{3}{s+4}$. Show the intermediate substitution and simplification.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a unit-step input, the cue is $X(s)=\\frac{1}{s}$, so we substitute this directly into the transfer-function output rule and keep only the algebraic transform form needed for inversion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=H(s)X(s)=\\left(\\frac{3}{s+4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{s}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\frac{3}{s(s+4)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This form is now inverse-Laplace ready and uses the same denominator structure as the tutorial baseline. The next reusable setup is a partial-fraction form for later inversion:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{3}{s(s+4)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Clearing denominators gives $3=A(s+4)+Bs$, so the coefficients are solved by coefficient matching in the next section. This preserves the established workflow: identify $H(s)$, substitute $1/s$, simplify $Y(s)$, then invert.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Step Response from a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding a Step Response from a First-Order Transfer Function",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $y(t)$ for $H(s)=\\frac{5}{s+2}$ with a unit-step input.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example adds one wrinkle to the prior substitution step: after forming $Y(s)$, we now carry the first-order expression through partial fractions so the inverse Laplace table can be applied directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a first-order transfer function under a unit-step input, so keep the same core move and start with the product form $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$ with $X(s)=\\frac{1}{s}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\left(\\frac{5}{s+2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{s}\\right)=\\frac{5}{s(s+2)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Decompose $\\frac{5}{s(s+2)}$ into standard inverse-Laplace pieces: $\\frac{5}{s(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5=A(s+2)+Bs",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match constants and slopes: $2A=5\\Rightarrow A=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $A+B=0\\Rightarrow B=-\\frac{5}{2}$, so $Y(s)=\\frac{5/2}{s}-\\frac{5/2}{s+2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{5}{s(s+2)}\\right\\}=\\frac{5}{2}\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\right\\}-\\frac{5}{2}\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+2}\\right\\}=\\frac{5}{2}(1-e^{-2t})u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the first-order step response is $y(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(1-e^{-2t})u(t)$, a stable DC value of $\\frac{5}{2}$ with an exponential transient set by the pole at $-2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Step Response from a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding a Step Response with Distinct Real Poles",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $y(t)$ when $H(s)=\\frac{12}{(s+1)(s+3)}$ and the input is $u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example keeps the same unit-step substitution rule and adds one new wrinkle: the transfer function now has two distinct real poles, so we must carry $Y(s)$ through a three-term partial-fraction expansion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the cue $X(s)=\\frac{1}{s}$ for a unit step and form the step-response transform from the product rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=H(s)X(s)=\\left(\\frac{12}{(s+1)(s+3)}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{s}\\right)=\\frac{12}{s(s+1)(s+3)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set up the decomposition into standard inverse-Laplace pieces with distinct real poles:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{12}{s(s+1)(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+1}+\\frac{C}{s+3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "12=A(s+1)(s+3)+Bs(s+3)+Cs(s+1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Evaluate the identity at the poles: $s=0 \\Rightarrow 12=3A \\Rightarrow A=4$, $s=-1 \\Rightarrow 12=-2B \\Rightarrow B=-6$, and $s=-3 \\Rightarrow 12=-6C \\Rightarrow C=2$. So $Y(s)=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{6}{s+1}+\\frac{2}{s+3}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{Y(s)\\}=4\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\right\\}-6\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}\\right\\}+2\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+3}\\right\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\left(4-6e^{-t}+2e^{-3t}\\right)u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is causal and stable with a step DC term $4u(t)$ plus two decays from poles at $-1$ and $-3$, matching the practice pattern for distinct real poles.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Step Response from a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Finding a Step Response with Repeated Poles",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the unit-step response for $H(s)=\\frac{4}{(s+1)^2}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This final example keeps the same two-step pipeline but introduces one new wrinkle: a repeated pole, so decomposition must include a repeated-denominator term before inversion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "As before, use the unit-step cue $X(s)=\\frac{1}{s}$ and write the step-response transform product.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=H(s)X(s)=\\left(\\frac{4}{(s+1)^2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{s}\\right)=\\frac{4}{s(s+1)^2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the repeated-pole form for partial fractions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{4}{s(s+1)^2}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+1}+\\frac{C}{(s+1)^2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4=A(s+1)^2+Bs(s+1)+Cs",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Clear denominators and solve coefficients: $4=A(s+1)^2+Bs(s+1)+Cs$, so $s=0\\Rightarrow A=4$, and $s=-1\\Rightarrow C=-4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the remaining linear term: from $2A+B+C=0$, with $A=4$, $C=-4$, we get $B=-4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{4}{s+1}-\\frac{4}{(s+1)^2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{Y(s)\\}=4\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\right\\}-4\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}\\right\\}-4\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{(s+1)^2}\\right\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\left(4-4e^{-t}-4te^{-t}\\right)u(t)=4\\left(1-(1+t)e^{-t}\\right)u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The repeated pole introduces the $te^{-t}$ term, so repeated roots should be handled with the correct additional decomposition term $\\frac{C}{(s+1)^2}$ and the corresponding $t e^{-t}$ inverse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This result preserves the same unit-step workflow and only adds the repeated-pole pattern needed for the next practice questions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
